      "text": "WRIGHT NEWS INTERNATIONAL 🌍\nTOURIST FALLS INTO ACTIVE VOLCANO — FAMILY CLAIMS SHE WAS ABANDONED BY GUIDE\n\nINDONESIA — A 26-year-old Brazilian tourist is trapped inside Mount Rinjani, an active volcano in Indonesia, after reportedly being left behind by her hiking guide.\n\nJuliana Marins fell over 1,600 feet into the volcano on Saturday morning while hiking with a small group. Authorities confirmed that she slipped from a cliffside trail overlooking the crater lake.\n\nHer sister told Brazilian outlet Fantástico that the guide abandoned Juliana after she stopped to rest due to fatigue. “They continued on, and the guide didn’t stay with her,” her sister Marianna said. When he returned later, it was too late — Juliana had vanished over the edge.\n\nDrone surveillance located Marins stuck motionless on a rock cliff approximately 500 meters deep. Rescuers initially reported hearing her screams for help, but poor weather, fog, and dangerous terrain have stalled the mission. Helicopter support has also been grounded due to unsafe flying conditions.\n\nGunung Rinjani National Park said rescuers were “pulled back to a safe position” for their own safety and that specialized mountaineers are now attempting a renewed effort to reach her.\n\nJuliana’s family has issued an emotional plea for international assistance, accusing Indonesian officials of giving false information and inconsistent updates. “We’re living in a nightmare,” a close friend said in a video statement.\n\nPhotos posted by Marins days before the accident show her backpacking, scuba diving, and hiking across Southeast Asia. She had been traveling through Vietnam, Thailand, and the Philippines as part of a long-awaited trip.\n\nThe Brazilian embassy in Jakarta is now assisting the family, but questions remain over the accountability of the tour operator and the decision to leave her alone.\n\nRescue teams are racing against time. As of Tuesday, Juliana’s exact condition remains unknown.\n\n#WRIGHTNEWSINTERNATIONAL\n#VolcanoFall #MountRinjani #Indonesia #JulianaMarins #TouristTragedy #TravelSafety",
